[Line "4.Bd3 Nf6 5.O-O O-O 6.c3 Nbd7 7.Re1 e5 8.Nbd2 b6 9.Nf1 Bb7"]
[Section "3.Nf3 d6 4.Bd3 (and 4.c3)"]
[Summary "White defends e4 with the bishop, castles and builds c3, Re1, Nbd2-f1-g3. Black plays the Pirc set-up ...Nf6, ...O-O, ...Nbd7 and breaks with ...e5; the bishop on b7 and the plan ...a6/...b5 or ...f5 follow."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nf3 { 46 of his games. The same position arises from 1.e4 g6 2.Nf3 Bg7 3.d4 d6 (14 games), and from 2.Nf3 Bg7 3.c3 or 3.Bc4 d6 followed by d4.
With the knight on f3 and no knight on c3, White has no easy way to build a big centre, so these lines are quieter than the Austrian or 150 Attacks. They are also where the Tiger idea ...a6 does not work: after 4.Be2 a6 White plays 5.c4! (the c-pawn is free, because no knight sits on c3), so Black simply plays the Pirc. }
3... d6 { [%cal Gg8f6,Ge8g8,Ge7e5] The pawn first, the knight afterwards: with ...d6 on the board White cannot answer ...Nf6 with e4-e5. Black now has the whole Pirc/King's Indian set-up in hand: ...Nf6, ...O-O, then ...e5 or ...c5 or ...c6 depending on what White does. }
4. Bd3 { Defends e4 with the bishop and keeps the knight free for d2, so that c3 can follow without a clash. The plan is O-O, c3, Re1 and Nbd2-f1-g3, a slow build-up that waits for ...e5. The elite database answers 4...Nf6 in 45% of the games and 4...Nc6 in 11% (see the next but one game). 4.c3 Nf6 5.Bd3 O-O 6.O-O Nbd7 is the same position. }
( 4. c3 { Just another move order (also reached via 2.Nf3 Bg7 3.c3 d6 4.d4): White prepares Bd3 or Qc2 and keeps the option of recapturing on d4 with the pawn. The elite games go 4...Nf6 (46%) and then 5.Bd3 (71%), and 4...Nf6 5.Bd3 O-O 6.O-O Nbd7 7.Re1 e5 8.Nbd2 is this game. See the game on 4.c3 Nc6 for the second option. } 4... Nf6 5. Bd3 O-O 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Re1 e5 8. Nbd2 )
4... Nf6 5. O-O O-O 6. c3 { [%cal Rg7d4] Takes the d4 square away from the bishop on g7: the pawn on d4 is now supported twice and ...e5 does not win a tempo on it. White's other sixth moves: 6.Re1 and 6.h3 come in the next games; 6.Nbd2 (471 elite games) and 6.Nc3 have no source here. After 6.Nbd2 the elite games go 6...Nc6 or 6...c5, and 6...e5 scores badly for Black (White won 16 of 22). }
6... Nbd7 { [%cal Gd7e5,Gd7c5,Ge7e5] Prepares ...e5 with the knight on d7 protecting e5 and keeping the c8-bishop for later. Compared with ...Nc6 it avoids d4-d5 with a gain of tempo on the knight. In the elite games 6...Nc6 is the more popular choice (49% against 9%) and is covered in a game of its own; Nbd7 is what the three Pirc sources give against this set-up. }
7. Re1 { Supports e4, so that after ...e5 White need not worry about the e-pawn. } ( 7. e5?! { Premature: it opens the centre when Black is better developed. } 7... dxe5 8. dxe5 Ng4 9. e6 fxe6 10. Qe2 Nde5 11. Nxe5 Nxe5 12. Be4 Nf7! { [%cal Gf7d6,Ge6e5] The knight comes back to protect the weak pawn on e6 and clear the way for ...e5; the second player is fine. } 13. Nd2 e5 14. Rd1 Nd6 15. Bc2 Bf5 )
7... e5 { [%cal Ge5d4] Black strikes before White has finished the set-up. The knight on d7 guards e5, so the pawn cannot simply be taken: d4 is under pressure, and exchanging on e5 is rare in the elite games (1%) because the symmetrical structure gives White nothing to play for. }
8. Nbd2 { The main move (52% in the elite games). The knight heads for f1 and g3, and it also unblocks the queen's bishop. } ( 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4 b6 10. Nbd2 Bb7 11. a4 a6 12. Qc2 Qe8 13. Rad1 Nh5! { [%cal Gh5f4,Gh5g3] Trades the dark-squared bishop that White has just posted on h4, and ...Qe7 follows. } 14. Bg3 Nxg3 15. hxg3 Qe7 )
( 8. h3 b6 9. Nbd2 Bb7 10. a4 a6 11. b4 Re8 12. Bb2 d5 { [%cal Gd5e4] The break arrives as soon as White spends time on the queenside. } 13. exd5 Nxd5 )
8... b6 { [%cal Gc8b7,Gb6b5] The knight on d7 blocks the c8-bishop, so the bishop goes round by b7, where it also hits e4. } 9. Nf1 Bb7 { [%cal Gf6e8,Gf7f5,Gb6b5,Ga7a6,Gf6h5,Gd6d5,Rf1g3,Rc1e3] Black has several plans and none of them is forced: a kingside advance with ...Nh5 and ...f5-f4 (the knight on f1 is on its way to g3, so ...f4 is often welcome), the break ...d5 when White's pieces are misplaced, or the queenside expansion ...a6 and ...b5. White's trump is the slow reorganisation Ng3, Bg5 or Be3 and Qd2. Roughly equal, and a very flexible position for the second player. } *

[Line "8...Re8 9.h3 b6 10.a4 a6 11.Nf1 Bb7 12.Ng3 d5"]
[Section "3.Nf3 d6 4.Bd3 (and 4.c3)"]
[Summary "The same set-up with the rook on e8 behind the e-pawn. Black waits for Ng3 and then breaks with ...d5; against the natural exchanges the centre opens in his favour."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nf3 d6 4. Bd3 Nf6 5. O-O O-O 6. c3 Nbd7 7. Re1 e5 8. Nbd2 Re8 { [%cal Ge8e4,Gd6d5] A second repertoire choice. The rook backs the centre, so that after ...d5 and ...exd4 the e-file opens with a tempo on the pinned pieces. The second player usually follows with ...b6, ...Bb7 and ...a6, as in the main game. }
9. h3 b6 10. a4 a6 { Stops Bb5 and Nb3 ideas and keeps the queenside closed to the a4-a5 lever. } 11. Nf1 Bb7 12. Ng3 { Now Ng3 protects e4 again and eyes f5 and h5, but Black has used the same moves to get ready for the break. } ( 12. d5 c6! { [%cal Gc6d5] Hits the pawn on d5 before White can consolidate. } 13. dxc6 Bxc6 14. Ng3 d5! { [%cal Gd5e4,Gc6e4] The same break, now with the bishop on c6 adding to the pressure on e4. } 15. exd5 Bxd5 16. Bc2 Qc7 )
12... d5! { [%cal Gd5e4,Ge5d4] The central break. All the white pieces are in the wrong places for the opened centre. } 13. exd5 { 13.dxe5 dxe4! is the other try, see the variation. }
( 13. dxe5 dxe4! { [%cal Gb7e4,Gd8d1] Opens the long diagonal for the b7-bishop and the d-file for the queen: after the exchanges on e4 and e5 Black's pieces are the more active. } 14. exf6 ( 14. Nxe4 Nxe5 15. Nxf6+ Bxf6 16. Nxe5 Rxe5 ) 14... Nxf6 15. Nxe4 Nxe4 16. Bf4 Nc5 )
13... exd4 14. c4 Rxe1+ 15. Qxe1 Nc5 { [%cal Gc5d3,Gc5b3] The knight lands on c5 and hits the d3-bishop. } 16. Bf1 c6 { [%cal Gc6d5] Black takes the pawn back on d5 and has the better pieces and the more active play: the pawn on d4 cramps White and the bishop on f1 is passive. A little better for Black. } *

[Line "6.Re1 Bg4 7.h3 Bxf3 8.Qxf3 Nc6 9.c3 e5 10.d5 Ne7 11.c4 Nd7 12.Nc3 f5"]
[Section "3.Nf3 d6 4.Bd3 (and 4.c3)"]
[Summary "Against 6.Re1 Black pins the knight and trades it, then attacks d4 with ...Nc6 and ...e5. After d4-d5 the position is a King's Indian with the c-pawn on c4 and the king's bishop strong: ...f5."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nf3 d6 4. Bd3 Nf6 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 { Defends e4 at once, so Black cannot count on ...Nxe4. The elite games answer with ...Nc6 (51%) or ...Bg4 (16%). 6...Nc6 7.c3 e5 reaches the Nc6 game below. }
6... Bg4 { [%cal Gg4f3,Ge7e5] The pin on f3 undermines the d4-pawn: ...e5 is next, and White has not yet played h3. } 7. h3 { The natural question to the bishop. } 7... Bxf3 { [%cal Gf3d1] The bishop does not retreat: with the knight gone d4 is only guarded by the queen and the bishop on g7 is the better minor piece. }
8. Qxf3 Nc6 { [%cal Gc6d4,Ge7e5] White needs to protect d4 at once. } 9. c3 { 9.d5 is a positional error: after 9...Nd4 the knight hits the queen and c2 and sits on a square White can hardly challenge. } 9... e5 { [%cal Ge5d4] } 10. d5 { If the centre stays closed the game becomes a King's Indian, and Black has the kingside play. 10.dxe5 dxe5 leaves the symmetrical structure with no weaknesses on either side. } 10... Ne7 { [%cal Ge7g6,Gf7f5,Gf6d7] } 11. c4 Nd7 { [%cal Gd7c5,Gd7f6,Gf7f5] Clears the f-pawn: ...f5 now strikes at e4. The knight on d7 can also drop back to f6 if the position needs closing. }
12. Nc3 f5 { [%cal Gf5e4,Gf5f4] Standard King's Indian play. The pawn storm is only advisable because the centre is closed: the bishop on d3 and the queen on f3 both look at the kingside, so every pawn move needs to be checked. In the model game (Ramirez) White tried 13.Qd1 f4 14.f3 g5 15.b4 h5 and a pawn race on opposite wings followed. Roughly equal; Black's play is the easier one to find, since the plan is clear. } *

[Line "Second option: 4.c3 Nc6 5.Bd3 e5 6.d5 Nce7 7.c4 f5 8.Nc3 Nf6"]
[Section "3.Nf3 d6 4.Bd3 (and 4.c3)"]
[Summary "The knight goes to c6 at once, the idea behind the Modern. After c3 and Bd3 the break ...e5 comes immediately, and d5 gives Black a King's Indian with ...f5 and ...Nf6 for free. The same position comes from 4.Bd3 Nc6 5.c3."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nf3 d6 4. c3 4... Nc6 { A natural second choice against 4.c3 (15% of the elite games) and 4.Bd3 (11%). Black does not need ...Nf6 first, because ...e5 is available at once. }
( 4... Nf6 { Also good, and the move he usually plays: after 5.Bd3 O-O 6.O-O Nbd7 the game transposes into the first game of this section. } 5. Bd3 O-O 6. O-O Nbd7 )
5. Bd3 ( 5. Bb5 a6 { White can also pin the knight and threaten d5, but the bishop is simply kicked out. } 6. Bxc6+ bxc6 { [%cal Gc8g4,Gg8f6] Black has the bishop pair and the doubled c-pawn supports d5 and e5. } )
5... e5 { [%cal Ge5d4] A direct strike with the knight already on c6: d4 is attacked twice, and White has to choose between d5, dxe5 and a quiet move. } 6. d5 { The most natural: it takes the tempo on the knight. } ( 6. dxe5 { Either recapture is fine. } 6... Nxe5 7. Nxe5 dxe5 { A position with no special features; ...Nf6, ...O-O and ...f5 are all possible, and White has no way of exploiting anything. } ) ( 6. Be3 Nf6 7. h3 O-O 8. O-O d5! { [%cal Gd5e4] Do not discard a pawn thrust just because it is not thematic: here it is well supported by the pieces, and White's Be3 and h3 are not placed to meet it. } )
6... Nce7 { [%cal Ge7g6,Gf7f5,Gg8f6] } 7. c4 f5 { [%cal Gf5e4] With the bishop on d3, the e4-pawn is the target. } 8. Nc3 ( 8. exf5 gxf5 { Always the pawn: the e5-pawn takes e4 and the bishop on g7 stays alive. } ) 8... Nf6 { [%cal Gb2b4,Ga7a5,Ge8g8,Gf5f4,Gg6g5,Gb7b6] The study rates this a little better for White on paper, but adds that Black scores better in practice: White has no clear plan, and Black gets a dream King's Indian attack. Stop White's queenside play with ...a5 (and ...b6), castle, and go for ...f4 and ...g5. In the elite games 5.c3 e5 6.d5 Nce7 7.c4 f5 8.Nc3 was played 31 times, and 4.Bd3 Nc6 5.c3 reaches it as well. } *

[Line "6.h3 c6 7.c3 Nbd7 8.Re1 e5 9.Nbd2 Qc7 10.Nf1 Re8 11.a4 a5 12.Qc2 d5"]
[Section "3.Nf3 d6 4.Bd3 (and 4.c3)"]
[Summary "When White takes g4 away with h3, the pawn goes to c6 first. Black builds ...Nbd7, ...Qc7, ...Re8 and strikes with ...e5 and ...d5, which works because no white knight sits on c3. The same position comes from 4.h3."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nf3 d6 4. Bd3 Nf6 5. O-O O-O 6. h3 { Takes g4 away, so ...Bg4 and ...Ng4 are gone. 4.h3 Nf6 5.Bd3 O-O 6.O-O reaches the same position, so 4.h3 (7 of his games) is dealt with here. Against 4.h3 Nf6 5.Nc3 the game transposes into the Pirc Classical (see the Nc3 chapter). }
6... c6 { [%cal Gc6d5,Gb8d7,Gd8c7,Gf8e8,Ge7e5] With the g4-square gone Black has no choice but this quiet move. The break ...e5 remains the aim, but it has to be prepared: ...Nbd7, ...Qc7 and ...Re8 first, so that White cannot meet it with a clean exchange. 6...Nc6 is also played often (49% in the elite games) and leads to the structure of the previous game after 7.c3. }
7. c3 Nbd7 8. Re1 e5 { Both sides are playing for the same break: whoever plays it first gets the better version of the centre. Black is on time here. } 9. Nbd2 Qc7 { [%cal Gc7e7,Gf8e8] The queen supports e5 and d6, and clears the back rank for the rook. } 10. Nf1 { [%cal Gf1g3] } 10... Re8 11. a4 a5 { Fixes the queenside before White uses a4-a5. } 12. Qc2 ( 12. dxe5 dxe5 13. Qc2 Nf8 14. Bg5 Ne6 { [%cal Ge6f4] } ) 12... d5! { [%cal Gd5e4,Gd7e5,Ge5d4] With no knight on c3 the e4-pawn has lost its main defender, so ...d5 is a pawn thrust that really works. A central push that the pieces support is never really bad, even in a Pirc structure, so do not discard it just because it is not thematic. } 13. exd5 Nxd5 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. Nxe5 Rxe5 16. Rxe5 Qxe5 17. Ne3 { Black could take on e3, but that would help White develop the dark-squared bishop. } 17... Be6 { [%cal Gd5e3,Ge6d5] Equal: the knight on d5 is well placed, the queen is centralised, and after 18.Nxd5 Bxd5 the bishop on d5 and the bishop on g7 both control long diagonals. } 18. Nxd5 Bxd5 *

[Line "4.Bc4 Nc6 5.O-O Nf6 6.Re1 O-O 7.h3 e5 8.d5 Ne7"]
[Section "3.Nf3 d6 4.Bc4"]
[Summary "The bishop eyes f7 and White prepares e5 with Qe2 or Re1. Black develops the knight first to c6, so that after ...Nf6 the pin ...Bg4 and the break ...e5 are available. The main line reaches a King's Indian with ...Ne7 and ...f5."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nf3 d6 4. Bc4 { 11 of his games, and the second most common move in the elite database (16%). White develops with a tempo on f7 and keeps e5 in reserve: with Re1 or Qe2 behind it, a pawn on e5 would take away Black's best squares. 4...Bg4? is not playable here: Bxf7+ Kxf7 Ng5+ and the queen takes the bishop that was just unmasked. }
4... Nc6 { [%cal Gc6d4,Gg8f6,Ge7e5] The choice of the first study, and also reached by 4...Nf6 5.Qe2 Nc6 6.O-O (see the next game). The knight goes to c6 before ...Nf6: from c6 it puts d4 under attack, so that ...Bg4 and ...e5 become real threats, and White's usual anti-Nf6 idea Qe2 is met by the pin. }
( 4... Nd7?? { Loses at once. } 5. Bxf7+! { The bishop takes, and the king is drawn out. } 5... Kf8 ( 5... Kxf7 6. Ng5+ Ke8 ( 6... Kf6 7. Qf3# ) 7. Ne6 ) 6. Ng5 )
5. O-O ( 5. d5 Nb8 { White can try to punish the knight at once, but the position is peaceful enough for it to retreat. The bishop gains the long diagonal and ...c6 and ...Nf6 follow. 5...Ne5 is also fine. } )
( 5. c3 Nf6 { This does not really transpose to the other lines. The break ...e5 is already possible, but ...Nf6 first is better. } 6. Qe2 { Here ...Bg4 is much weaker than after O-O: d4 is not hanging, because c3 has been played, and White can kick the bishop away. } ( 6. Nbd2 e5 7. dxe5 Nxe5 ) 6... e5 7. dxe5 Nxe5 { Taking back with the knight scores better in practice than with the pawn, and the position is equal but dull; there is no clear plan for either side, so patience is needed. } )
5... Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4] Hits e4, and White has to defend it. If White simply develops with Nc3, the game transposes to the Mad Dog of the Nc3 chapter after ...O-O. } 6. Re1 ( 6. Qe2 Bg4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc6d4] The pin works here, because the d4-pawn is hanging and White has no time to chase the bishop with h3 or Nd2. } 7. c3 e5 8. d5 ( 8. Rd1 O-O 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 Nd7 11. Be3 Kh8 { [%cal Gf7f5] } ) 8... Ne7 { [%cal Gf7f5,Gf6d7,Ge8g8,Rg4f3,Rh2h3] } )
( 6. e5!? { Equal, but a different structure: White has to give up a pawn. } 6... dxe5 7. dxe5 Qxd1 8. Rxd1 Ng4 { [%csl Gg4,Gg7,Gc6,Re5] } 9. e6 Bxe6! { Important: take with the bishop. } )
6... O-O { [%cal Ge7e5] } 7. h3 { Stops Bg4, but the break is ready. } 7... e5! { [%cal Ge5d4] Black strikes at the centre as always. } 8. d5 ( 8. dxe5 { Forces exchanges. Either recapture is fine; dxe5 seems to score a bit better in practice. } 8... dxe5 9. Qxd8 Rxd8 { Black is fine, and the pleasant endgame has no weaknesses. } )
8... Ne7 { [%cal Gf6d7,Gf7f5,Gc7c6] The usual King's Indian choice: the knight goes to e7, where it supports ...f5. Black can attack on the kingside with ...Nd7 and ...f5, or play in the centre with ...c6. Roughly equal. } *

[Line "Second option: 4...Nf6 5.Qe2 Nc6 6.O-O Bg4 7.c3 e5 8.d5 Ne7"]
[Section "3.Nf3 d6 4.Bc4"]
[Summary "His own 4...Nf6. After 5.Nc3 the game is in the Mad Dog of the Nc3 chapter; after 5.Qe2 Black plays ...Nc6 and reaches the previous game, because 5...O-O is worse."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nf3 d6 4. Bc4 Nf6 { Still the natural move (47% in the elite games), and with it Black keeps his usual set-up. 5.Nc3 O-O 6.O-O Nc6 is the Mad Dog of the Nc3 chapter. The delicate move is 5.Qe2, which prepares e5. }
5. Qe2 { Defends e4 and prepares e4-e5, after which ...dxe5 dxe5 would give White the d-file. 965 elite games. } 5... Nc6 { [%cal Gc6d4,Gc8g4] With 6.O-O the position is the one reached from 4...Nc6 5.O-O Nf6 6.Qe2 in the previous game: the pin ...Bg4 follows and White cannot easily chase the bishop away. 6.e5 is the other try (18%): Black takes dxe5 and has done well in the elite games, but the continuation 7.dxe5 is not covered by a source. }
( 5... O-O?! { The natural move, but after the central break the position is a little better for White. } 6. O-O Bg4 { Pinning the knight, and asking White to defend the pawn on d4. } 7. e5! { Hits the knight on f6 before Black has castled kingside into safety; after ...dxe5 dxe5 the knight has to go to d5 (the only move in all 46 elite games), and White's pieces are the better placed. } 7... dxe5 8. dxe5 Nd5 9. Nbd2 { [%cal Gd2e4,Gf3d4] } 9... Nb6 10. Bd3 Nc6 11. h3 Nd4 12. Qe4 Nxf3+ 13. Nxf3 Bxf3 14. Qxf3 c6 { The pawn on e5 shuts in the bishop on g7 and White has the more active pieces. The study rates this a small plus for White, which is the reason to prefer 5...Nc6. } )
6. O-O Bg4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc6d4] } 7. c3 { Defends d4 at once. } 7... e5 8. d5 ( 8. Rd1 O-O 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 Nd7 11. Be3 Kh8 { [%cal Gf7f5] } ) 8... Ne7 { [%cal Gf7f5,Gf6d7,Ge8g8,Rg4f3,Rh2h3] Black keeps the bishop on g4 as long as possible. The plan is ...Nd7 (or ...O-O) followed by ...f5. If White plays h3 the bishop takes on f3, and the queen's position is then spoilt. } *

[Line "4.Be3 Nf6 5.Nc3 c6 6.Qd2 b5 7.Bd3 Nbd7 8.O-O Qc7 9.Bh6 O-O 10.Bxg7 Kxg7 11.a4 b4 12.Ne2 c5 13.dxc5 Qxc5"]
[Section "4.Be3: the 150 Attack with Nf3"]
[Summary "With Nf3 instead of f3 White cannot storm the kingside as fast, so Black plays the Czech set-up ...c6, ...b5, ...Nbd7, ...Qc7 and castles when the dark-squared bishops are about to be traded. Draft: one study only."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nf3 d6 4. Be3 { 7 of his games. The 150 Attack with the knight on f3: White wants Qd2 and Bh6 to trade Black's fianchettoed bishop, but without f3 and g4 the attack is slower than in the lines with f3. After 4...Nf6 White plays 5.Nc3 in 98% of the elite games, so this is the Pirc 150 Attack. }
4... Nf6 5. Nc3 c6 { [%cal Gb7b5,Gb8d7,Gd8c7,Gd8a5] The study answers the 150 Attack with the Czech set-up: ...c6 and ...b5, then ...Nbd7 and ...Qc7 or ...Qa5, with the king staying flexible. In the elite games 5...O-O is the main move (52%) and 5...c6 follows (19%); only the one study gives a line, see the notes. }
6. Qd2 b5 { [%cal Gb5b4,Gd8a5] Black expands on the queenside while White prepares his kingside play. } 7. Bd3 Nbd7 { [%cal Gd7b6] } 8. O-O Qc7 { [%cal Gc7c5,Gb5b4] Getting ready for play on the queenside. An alternative is ...Qa5, which a strong grandmaster has played. } 9. Bh6 O-O { Black castles now: the attack with the king on the kingside is no longer promising once the dark-squared bishops are traded. } 10. Bxg7 Kxg7 { A quiet position. White has castled as well, so neither side can attack the king. } 11. a4 b4 12. Ne2 c5 { Black plays on in the centre and on the queenside. } 13. dxc5 Qxc5 *

[Line "4.Be2 Nf6 5.Nc3 O-O 6.O-O a6: into the Nc3 chapter"]
[Section "4.Be2, 4.Nc3, 4.h3 and the other transpositions"]
[Summary "Most other fourth moves lead to Nc3 and the Pirc Classical, which is the Nc3 chapter: with Nf3 played and Nc3 added, Black plays his usual ...Nf6, ...O-O and ...a6."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nf3 d6 4. Be2 { Only 3 of his games. White moves into the Classical without committing the c-pawn. The position after 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 O-O 6.O-O is the one reached by 3.Nc3 d6 4.Be2 Nf6 5.Nf3, a line of the Nc3 chapter. }
( 4. Nc3 { The Two Knights or Classical Variation, 5 of his games: the Nc3 chapter, where 4...a6 and 4...Nf6 are both covered. } 4... Nf6 5. Be2 O-O 6. O-O a6 )
( 4. h3 { 4.h3 (7 of his games) takes g4 away in advance. 4...Nf6 5.Bd3 O-O 6.O-O c6 reaches the position of the 6.h3 c6 game above. 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 O-O is the Pirc with h3, the Schlechter Variation, again in the Nc3 chapter. } 4... Nf6 5. Bd3 O-O 6. O-O c6 )
4... Nf6 { 98% of the elite games. } ( 4... a6?! { With 4.Be2 White has not committed the c-pawn, so 5.c4! is possible: the Modern-Defence authors warn that Black has been move-ordered into a Pirc where White picks the better centre. Play 4...Nf6 and avoid the problem. } 5. c4 )
5. Nc3 O-O 6. O-O a6 { [%cal Gb7b5,Gb8c6] The same move as in the Nc3 chapter: against a quiet bishop move ...b5 is available, and ...Nc6 and ...e5 stay in reserve. 6...c6, played by Lakdawala and Browne, is the other option. } *

[Line "2.Nf3 Bg7 3.Bc4 d6 4.O-O Nf6 5.Nc3 O-O 6.d4 Nc6: the Mad Dog"]
[Section "2.Nf3 Bg7: 3.Bc4, 3.c3 and 3.d3"]
[Summary "When White plays 2.Nf3 and 3.Bc4 without d4, Black keeps to ...d6, ...Nf6 and ...O-O. Almost every line comes back to 4.Bc4 or 4.d4, and the quiet 4.O-O Nf6 5.Nc3 O-O 6.d4 is the Mad Dog."]

1. e4 g6 2. Nf3 { 14 of his games reach the Nf3 lines by 2.Nf3 Bg7 3.d4 d6 (see the first game). Other third moves are met by ...d6 too: after 3.c3 d6 4.d4 Nf6 5.Bd3 the game is in the 4.c3 line; the quiet 3.d3 has no source. } 2... Bg7 3. Bc4 { 9 of his games. In the elite database the main answer is 3...c5 (70%), which is sharper and has no source here. Black keeps to the Pirc set-up, since the move orders come back to the same positions. } 3... d6 4. O-O { A slow move: the bishop and king wait, with d4 still to come. } ( 4. d4 { Transposes to the 4.Bc4 game above: 4...Nf6 5.Qe2 Nc6 is the main test, and 5.Nc3 O-O 6.O-O Nc6 is the Mad Dog. } 4... Nf6 )
4... Nf6 5. Nc3 O-O 6. d4 Nc6 { The Mad Dog of the Nc3 chapter, by transposition: with the pawn on d4 and the knights on c3 and f3 it is exactly the position 3.Nc3 d6 4.Bc4 Nf6 5.Nf3 O-O 6.O-O Nc6. } *
